hey everyone welcome to this week's  edition of After Effects tips and tricks  I'm Cameron and this is motion science  today I want to talk a little bit about  the 3d camera I've had a whole lot of  response really great response from the  3d camera techniques video that I put up  on YouTube and the website motion  science TV the responses has been great  it's been up for about a year now but  it's just now starting to get a lot of  attention so I thought this week would  be kind of cool to talk a little more  about the 3d camera and another function  of the 3d camera that some of you may  know about beginners probably don't know  about but I want to show you so what I  have here is a video clip in my dining  room that I shot on my iPhone 7 and it's  this pretty cool statue I've got I've  got a bride and groom this is the bride  and she's just sitting on the table and  what I wanted to show you is that you  can shoot video or you can take any  video that's already been shot and you  can easily track the footage and apply  graphics to it but I'm also going to  take it one step beyond that so this is  two parts so I'm gonna go through this  kind of quick I'm trying to keep these  videos fairly short so what I'm going to  do first is all you do is you bring in  your footage I imported it here I  brought it to a new composition here it  is  iPhone 7 footage I just click once on  the piece of footage go up to animation  track camera and what its gonna do is  after effects starts tracking the data  from the camera and it's tracking to try  and solve a 3d camera from the footage  so depending on how long the clip is  this can go quickly or it can take a  little bit of time what's cool about  this effect as well is that it analyzes  in the background  so you can see here it says analyzing  background so I could actually go and  work on  things in my After Effects project file  while this is running and I don't just  have to sit here and wait for it to be  done so up here you can see the  percentage it's 54 percent of the way  it's on frame 185 24 seconds remaining  yadda yadda yadda I'm gonna pause it  real quick and come back ok so it's  finished analyzing but now it's solving  the camera and boom there it is the  analyzing and solving is done and here's  all the track points now we can see in  the footage pretty cool stuff  one thing you want to check whenever you  track a camera is you want to go into  advanced and you want to look right here  at the average error you can see it says  0.99 pixels I always aim for one pixel  or below for the average area error if  you get up in like 3 4 5 pixel errors  it's not gonna look too great sometimes  2 pixel errors can be ok but typically I  try to stay below 1 pixel hair error if  it goes and it comes above 1 pixel  sometimes you can click detailed  analysis and it'll realized in more  detail and sometimes you'll get a better  average error sometimes you'll get a  worse average error you can also click  on auto detect and click one of these  options sometimes and make sure tracking  better sometimes it doesn't it just kind  of depends it's all trial and error with  this tracker so anyways I've got the  footage tracked and I'm just going to go  up here I'm not gonna go into too much  detail about what this can do but if you  see here there's three points right here  and if I go in between them see that  circle so if I click to highlight them  and then I right-click I can click I can  select one of these options I am going  to select a shadow catcher camera and  light so what I've got now is a shadow  catcher  a light that we can't see and a 3d  camera so I'm going to go in here I'm  going to select this object I used in  the 3d camera techniques video I'm gonna  paste it in here I am going to parent  the footage to the shadow catcher and  when I hold down shift and parent watch  where it's the watch the graphics snap  to the shadow catcher see that so now  it's took it's taken on the rotation the  orientation so watch this so now it  looks like the graphic is actually  sitting on top of a statue so the  graphic is 3d and it's taking advantage  of the 3d camera that's in the scene  then we run out of the chair  oh yeah there you go this is a real time  pretty believable  pretty cool so there you go it's a  pretty easy way to add graphics to  footage that you shoot so let me let me  take this one step further now so you  saw we can do by just adding the  graphics in well I'm gonna jump back  into this comp here this is a comp that  was in 3d camera techniques where I was  talking about the orbit camera and you  may remember this from the video series  but essentially I talked about the  different types of cameras in that you  can simulate with After Effects camera  and this is the orbit so pretty cool  move but but an interesting like if you  wanted to really add some interesting  camera movement to your animation and  let's say you wanted a handheld camera  look in your animation well you know  that you can take the camera you can  click on the position property and you  can add a wiggle expression to this  property I talked about this in the 3d  camera video but if I add a wiggle  okay so I've added a wiggle expression  to the position property and we can do  more things with the wiggle expression  but to make it look better but you'll  see here so like let's say we're just  trying to emulate a simple handheld  camera move this does an okay job and  like I said I can separate dimensions of  the position and make it look better but  another way to approach this is to take  the camera that we tracked this one and  apply this camera motion to that graphic  element so all I have to do to  accomplish that is to copy my shadow  catcher which effectively I'm treating  it as a null and my 3d camera I'm gonna  copy those two I'm gonna turn off this  camera I'm gonna paste my camera and my  null and turn this off  so right here we we lose the graphic  because of the camera movement so an  easy way to fix this is to parent the  camera to the shadow catcher and then  I'm going to reposition the shadow  catcher to match like where the graphic  is at so 960 by 540 and it looks like  it's at negative 250 there we go  and then I'm also going to zero out the  orientation properties  on going to position it so my scenes in  there around a little bit and see where  I'm at here  I'm also going to change the position  zero bring it closer maybe 250 maybe 500  just trying to Center it up here turn  off my Knoll and I'm gonna Ram or I'm  gonna preview this and see what looks  like okay so here you go so this is a  camera movement on my graphic which I  mean again it just looks really it looks  real looks like it doesn't look like a  simulated CG camera it just looks like a  real camera shooting a graphic so  there's a lot of applications for this  and I just wanted to show you just the  general idea so you got kind of get what  I'm talking about here just it opens up  a new possibility for what you can do  with the 3d camera go out and shoot some  real footage track it and then it apply  it to your graphic scenes and see what  you come up with so that's it for today  I hope you enjoyed this lesson this  quick lesson and until next time I'm  Cameron and this is motion science  [Music]
 

